From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Davi <davilimacarv2536@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 12:02:28 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] random source getrandom

On Linux, WTF's and bmalloc's cryptographically random sources draw
every value from `getrandom` and keep no state in the process: no
`/dev/urandom` descriptor and no ARC4 stream. Both used to be created
on first use and kept for the life of the process, which assumes that
the process that opened them is the one that reads them. An embedder
that forks with `clone3` and then closes the descriptors it did not
hand to the child breaks that assumption twice. The child inherits
`RandomDevice`'s descriptor number, closes it, and gives the number to
the next file it opens, so the next stir of the ARC4 stream reads that
file. A closed descriptor or an io_uring ring crashes the child, a
nonblocking eventfd spins on `EAGAIN` with the generator's lock held,
and a regular file yields predictable text and then loops forever at
its end. A sandboxed child cannot reopen `/dev/urandom` to recover.
Every child also inherits its parent's ARC4 state, so sibling children
hand out the same numbers until each one stirs again, 1,600,000 bytes
of draws later: the same `createVersion4UUIDString` values, and the
same seed for `VM::random` after each collection.

`RandomDevice::cryptographicallyRandomValues` calls
`getrandom(buffer, length, 0)` on Linux, retrying on `EINTR` and short
reads and crashing on any other failure. `cryptographicallyRandomNumber`
and `cryptographicallyRandomValues` call it directly instead of reading
the ARC4 stream, and bmalloc's `cryptoRandom`, which places the
Gigacage, does the same, so the parent holds no stray descriptor
either. The callers are cold (VM and global object creation, the start
of each collection, UUIDs, Gigacage setup), so a system call per draw
costs little. The embedder cannot get this from outside: the
descriptor and the stream are private statics with no reset, and a
reseed after each fork would hold only while every fork path remembered
to call it. Other platforms keep the ARC4 stream.
